// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// specs/t27os/ladder_serve.t27 -- the t27-native serve loop of the T27 Ladder (gHashTag/t27#13039).
// The Ladder is a loopback service in front of the free model pool: one POST /v1/chat/completions walks the
// rung table of ladder.t27 until one rung answers 200, GET /health lists the table. Until now the running
// service was a hand-written, untracked Node file, so a merged spec change (PR 13035 dropped DeepSeek) never
// reached it. This file owns what that file did: the accept loop, the request parse, the per-call timeout and
// the walk, and it reads the rung names, endpoints and models from ladder.t27 -- nothing here keeps a copy.
//
// Laws of ladder.t27 followed here: law 3 (the 503 names every rung that was tried, with its class), law 6
// (thinking off on the zai GLM head unless the client asked for it), law 7 (service noise never demotes; only
// 429/402 do), law 8 (a rung without a key or without a probed 200 makes no network call and is recorded as
// unwired). ladder_speed.t27: the walk starts at the last good rung (sticky) and the first two wired rungs are
// hedged -- both fire, the first 200 wins and the other call is killed.
//
// Shape: the pure fns below (ls_* , tested) own every byte decision -- the request head, the JSON member the
// rewrite replaces, the curl config, the reply split, the key rotation, the health and 503 bodies. The host
// half (ld_* , after them) only moves bytes: bind 127.0.0.1, poll, accept, spawn /usr/bin/curl for the
// upstream call (t27 has no TLS client; glass_window.t27 and keys_host.t27 spawn tools the same way), read the
// Keychain by account name through keys_host.kc_get_n. A key is read when a call needs it, goes to curl on a
// pipe (never argv, never a file), and its copy is wiped at once; no key value is ever logged.
//
// Known limits, stated so they are not found by surprise: one client connection is served at a time (the
// hedge runs two UPSTREAM calls at once, not two clients); a request body is capped at REQ_CAP and a reply at
// REPLY_CAP; JSON is checked structurally (one object, balanced), not fully validated; a rung's noise rest
// (NOISE_REST_S) is not kept -- a noisy rung is simply tried again on the next request; keys are rescanned
// every KEY_RESCAN_MS, not live.
//
// Keys by Keychain account name only (service ai.t27.os; keys.t27 keychain_account_n). Never print one.
// phi^2 + 1/phi^2 = 3 | TRINITY

module T27osLadderServe;

